\f0\fs48\b\cf1 Chonkstep themes\b0\cf0\par
\fs24 This capture uses a \b built-in\b0 Chonkstep theme. The compositor did not change, and no custom theme was written. Wallpaper follows the theme.\par
\fs32\b\cf3 Built in, and different from canopy\b0\cf0\par
\fs24\pard\li360
\bullet  \i sf-canopy\i0  is the theme that was already on the desk: night forest, constellation.\par
\bullet  \b\cf2 sf-wafer\cf0\b0  is the one used for the shot: light field, square pads, indigo accent.\par
\bullet  The rest of the set stays available: nextstep-classic, obsidian, washi, beos, system-7-classic, sf-dieshot, and the others the running binary lists.\par
\pard\li0\fs32\b Revert\b0\par
\fs22\f1 desk_m1.sh theme sf-canopy\f0\fs24\par
That is the same request the compositor takes as \f1 --desktop-request theme sf-canopy\f0, with \f1 XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/private/var/run/gpu-desktop\f0 and \f1 WAYLAND_DISPLAY=wayland-0\f0.\par
\fs32\b\cf1 Menu strip\b0\cf0\par
\fs24 TextEdit keeps this in-window menu. Terminal's strip is hidden for the shot, and only Terminal's: the desk's app-menu socket takes \f1 menubar hide\f0 for that process. Show it again with \f1 menubar show\f0 on the same socket. The compositor was not relaunched.\par
